  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how a complex crystal structure can be simplified into a framework description that highlights where lithium ions sit. It starts with a three-dimensional arrangement of polyhedra, then reduces this to a two-dimensional network built from anions and non-lithium cations. In this intermediate view, yellow spheres form the anion lattice, while red and pink sites mark the other cations embedded in the framework; green spheres indicate lithium.\n\nThe central idea is that the chemically complicated structure can be separated into a relatively regular anion framework and the voids or channels created by that framework. Once only the anion network is retained, the geometry of possible lithium positions becomes clearer. The final panel emphasizes this by shading connected regions that represent the topology of lithium sites supplied by the framework. Lithium appears to occupy a subset of these available positions within repeating motifs, while the non-lithium cations remain part of the host scaffold.\n\nOverall, the figure shows a progression from full crystal chemistry to an abstract structural map, revealing how the underlying anion framework determines the arrangement and connectivity of lithium sites.",
